  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 7

Iteration 6 ended: *the cliff is moved, not removed. Proportional spilling is
the next piece, and there is now a workload on each side of it.* This is that,
and a wrong-code bug found while doing it.

## The cliff

Running out of registers rejected the whole function, and the fallback gave
every value its own stack slot. One value past the pool cost the same as a
hundred. The scan now evicts one value at a time -- the one wanted furthest in
the future -- and leaves the rest where they are.

`kernel_deep.kotoba`: twenty-four independent lanes, above the pool on both
targets. 21 rotated samples, same amu, differing only in the allocator:

| workload | ISA | before | after | |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| kernel, kernel_wide | both | — | — | byte-identical |
| **kernel_deep** | **AArch64** | **3404 B / 81.6 ns** | **1096 B / 14.9 ns** | **5.5x** |
| **kernel_deep** | **x86-64** | **6545 B / 116.5 ns** | **2202 B / 34.1 ns** | **3.4x** |

Against LLVM, AArch64 native: **1.44x**, from 7.87x.

## Two sessions, one feature, and the difference between them

A concurrent session landed the same feature on kotoba-mir main while this one
was measuring. Its implementation is tighter than mine -- two slots on the
eleven-value program where mine took three -- and it is what main now carries.

**It also appended the spill store where the register ran out.** This allocator
walks one flat instruction list containing labels and branches, so that point
can sit inside one arm of a branch while the reload lands in the other: a slot
written on a path that was not taken, read on the path that was.

Executed on an M4, twelve values defined before an `if` and read only in the
arm that runs:

```
expected  282
main      6171913639     :status :ok, no trap
```

**Its entire test suite was green.** Every check of the allocator was
structural -- frame slots, encodings, byte strings -- and the arithmetic of a
taken branch is not structural.

The fix is to splice the store in after the definition. A definition dominates
every use of its value, because the program is in SSA form. Landed on top of
their implementation rather than in place of it.

## Meta-review

- **The failure mode of this iteration was not a wrong number. It was a green
  suite.** Nothing was skipped, nothing errored, no check was disabled. The
  checks that existed could not see the thing.
- **A test of this has to make the allocator run out of registers somewhere
  that does not dominate.** The first version of my own regression test passed
  against the broken build: its pressure peaked before the branch, so the
  hazard never arose. I nearly shipped a test of nothing under a name that says
  otherwise -- the third time in three iterations
  (iteration 4's benchmark, iteration 6's callee-saved harness, this).
- **The guard that works is the one that runs the program.** amu already had a
  native execution harness; the new test compiles and executes on both ISAs and
  was confirmed to fail against the build in question, returning 422139889607675
  on one and 14805267367 on the other.
- **Concurrent work on the same thing is not waste if the two disagree
  somewhere.** Mine was slower and correct on placement; theirs was tighter and
  wrong on it. Neither review would have produced the other's half. What found
  the bug was not review — it was having a program whose answer I knew.
- Also dropped a second victim search with an empty protected set, which could
  evict an operand of the instruction being allocated. Unreachable in both
  suites today; rejecting sends the function to the conservative path, which is
  slower and correct.

## Next

The allocator is no longer the largest term on any workload measured here. The
open items are named by earlier iterations and unchanged: self-tail calls past
the fuel prologue (iteration 5), and the fact that `allocate-with-spills` still
exists for functions containing calls, which have never been measured at all.
**Every ratio in this file is from a leaf.**"}]
